// D410 — TUPLE-DESTRUCTURE width preservation. A sized/unsigned element type declared on a
// tuple-pattern `let` annotation (`ro (a, b) (uint, uint) = (<lit>, <lit>)`) MUST flow into the
// per-element bindings, exactly as a record-field / call-argument literal coerces to its declared
// type (D55). It does NOT today: codegen's tuple-destructure path (`emit_stmt` → `emit_tuple_
// destructure`) DROPS `decl.ty` and types each element via `infer_expr_c_type(<IntLit>)`, which
// returns the signed wide default `nova_int` (emit_c.rs `IntLit => "nova_int"`). So the annotation
// `(uint, uint)` is silently discarded and a bare unsigned high-bit literal `0x8000000000000000`
// (2^63) is bound as `nova_int a = ((nova_int)-9223372036854775808LL)` — NEGATIVE. This extends the
// KNOWN tuple-literal-element collapse gap documented in d55_sized_literal_contexts.nv (the typed-
// RETURN position) to the DESTRUCTURE/let-annotation position.
//
// DISCRIMINATING design: the result binding is UNANNOTATED (`ro got = a`) so it inherits the
// element's INFERRED C-type. If the annotation collapsed the element to signed `nova_int`, the
// 2^63 bit-pattern reads as -2^63 → `got > 100` is FALSE (RUN-FAIL); a width-preserving `uint`
// lower keeps it positive → TRUE. Each unsigned-collapse assert uses a `> 100` MAGNITUDE check
// (NOT a round-trip `== 0x8000…`, which would collapse SYMMETRICALLY on both sides of `==` and
// mask the bug). The control test destructures `uint` IDENTIFIERS (not literals): codegen reads
// their preserved `nova_uint` from `var_types`, so it PASSES — proving the collapse is specific to
// bare-literal elements under a dropped annotation, not destructuring in general.
// Spec: spec/decisions/02-types.md (D55 literal coercion / uint≠int). Single `spec_tests.
// conformance` folder-module; all top-level names D410-prefixed to avoid CU-wide collisions.
module inprogress.d410_tuple_destructure_sized

// ── DISCRIMINATING #1: first-position unsigned high-bit literal under a `(uint, uint)` pattern
//    annotation. The annotation is dropped → element binds as signed `nova_int` = -2^63. ──
test "tuple-destructure: 1st-position uint literal keeps high-bit, no signed collapse (D410)" {
    ro (a, b) (uint, uint) = (0x8000000000000000, 1)   // 2^63 ; annotation MUST reach `a`
    ro got = a                  // UNANNOTATED: inherits a's inferred type
    assert(got > 100)           // uint: 2^63 > 100 ✓ ; signed collapse → -2^63 → FALSE (RUN-FAIL)
    assert(b == 1)              // 2nd element round-trips its small value
}

// ── DISCRIMINATING #2: the collapse is per-position — a high-bit literal in the SECOND slot
//    collapses too. `> 100` magnitude check (a symmetric `== 0x80…` would mask the bug). ──
test "tuple-destructure: 2nd-position uint literal also preserves width (D410)" {
    ro (a, b) (uint, uint) = (1, 0x8000000000000000)   // high-bit in slot 1
    ro hi = b                   // UNANNOTATED: inherits b's inferred type
    assert(hi > 100)            // uint: 2^63 > 100 ✓ ; signed collapse → negative → FALSE
    assert(a == 1)              // 1st element round-trips
}

// ── CONTROL: destructure of `uint` IDENTIFIERS (not literals). Codegen types each element from
//    `var_types` (preserved `nova_uint`), so width survives → PASSES. Demonstrates the collapse
//    is literal-specific (annotation-drop), NOT a destructure-wide defect. ──
test "tuple-destructure of uint identifiers preserves width (control, D410)" {
    ro hi uint = 0x8000000000000000   // 2^63 — correctly `nova_uint` (D130)
    ro lo uint = 1
    ro (a, b) = (hi, lo)              // elements are typed Idents → width preserved
    ro got = a                        // inherits a's nova_uint type
    assert(got > 100)                 // 2^63 > 100 — holds for the width-preserving control
    assert(b == 1)
}
